Big Tech is no longer just visiting Mangaluru, It’s moving in.

In January, Deloitte promised it would come to Mangaluru. This month, that commitment became a reality.

When Deloitte South Asia CEO Romal Shetty spoke at TiEcon Mangaluru, he highlighted our engineering talent, medical talent, and real estate, noting that an entry was only a matter of time.

Seeing its logo go up turns that early conversation into a reality.

That milestone leads a story Mangaluru is writing in three distinct acts: Arrive. Scale. Get acquired.

Arrive. Deloitte marks its initial footprint on the coast, setting the stage for what comes next.

Scale. NTT DATA entered Mangaluru through its acquisition of Niveus Solutions and currently has about 900 people here. By the end of 2027, it plans to go past 2,500, focusing on AI, cloud, and data. Companies do not triple a local workforce unless the city is consistently delivering.

Get acquired.  Morgan Stanley, one of the world's largest financial institutions, has agreed to acquire Vinyl Equity, a company founded in 2022 whose core engineering team is based in Mangaluru. Software engineered by a team on our coast is joining Morgan Stanley at Work, a platform serving over 3,400 corporate clients, in a deal expected to close in Q1 2027.

One global leader establishing a presence. One tripling its technical team. One being acquired by a Wall Street institution.

Deloitte’s arrival is just the opening signal. Which chapter do you think Mangaluru writes next?

