During its FQ226 earnings call, SoftBank noted that it is not defaulting on turbine orders and the company ‘[remains] focused and on track to execute [its] AI infrastructure plans’. The Ohio project is on track, but the contract is being revised.

In terms of implications for GEV, we highlight:

The news is a negative, as it marks a major Datacenter developer re-thinking its plans for power equipment. There are clearly negative implications for the broader AI complex within EE/MI;
GEV was on the initial list of companies that are involved in the Piketon, OH project, from March 2026 (see here). GEV themselves do not comment on specific customers or projects, understandably;
In terms of the scale of the OH project, it seems to have targeted 10GW of capacity. This comprises 9.2 GW of new natural gas generation and the estimated investment in the power generation component is ~$33bn. (For context, GEV’s overall SRA + Orders backlog balance is guided at >130GW at the end of 2026);
It is hard to delineate the precise nature of the comments from SoftBank / what they mean for the future of the project - the management team on the call today said that the company’s AI infrastructure projects are ‘on track’;
The period that SoftBank booked the charge for overlaps exactly with GEV’s Q3 results that were reported several weeks ago. There seemed to be nothing untoward / one off in those sets of GEV results, that would tie to the SoftBank charge;
In the background, it may be worth noting that OH is one of the states that ‘flipped’ politically in the Elections last week;
In the event that a customer delays some turbine orders / deliveries, we think it is highly likely that GEV could use those slots for other customers, as the company is booking Power orders for 2031 delivery.

